A||||21||batboy||batmobile update
Z||000000||batboy||07-10-99||10:11 PM||||I just now finished swapping front Grand Am disk brakes onto the front of my V8 Fiero. While this swap was not exactly easy, you need to be an experienced shade tree mechanic and know your way around brakes, it was not overly hard either.<p>Testing it out on the gravel road next to my house, the front brakes lock up great, but the smaller back brakes don't lock up now at low speeds on gravel. I will do the swap on the rear brakes next. The e-brake cable is rusted solid anyway, there is no vehicle inspections here, and the car has an automatic transmission anyway.<p>Total cost for the whole 1988 Grand Am to 1985 Fiero brake swap, front and rear, is approximately $400 which includes American made rotors and OEM rebuilt calipers which cost more money. I compared the Chinese rotors with the American rotors and decided to spend the extra $10 more each, there was a visible difference in quality. I also replaced all the wheel bearings, flexible brake hoses, and bought premium semi-metallic brake pads. A guy could do this a lot cheaper if he really wanted to. Lastly, I'm keeping my old calipers as backups for my other Fiero, so I didn't have any core returns ($10 per caliper).<p>Two things bothered me before I started, having the proper machine work done on the old rotors to turn them into hubs and the how much modification of the caliper mounting bracket was needed to clear the new rotor? This was not real clear to me as I read and reread the instructions for the swap.<p>Greendano helped me with the hubs, I would not have started this project if it wasn't for him. I used a 4 inch hand grinder on the bracket, no problem, I just went slow and trial fitted the rotor numerous times. A few tweaks of the dust cover with vice grips and a hammer and everything went together great.<p>Looks great too, like it's a factory job. I painted the calipers with Dupli-Color's 1200 degree high heat paint with ceramic. We'll see how this stuff holds up (it sprayed on real nice and easy), it was only five bucks a can and I have lots of paint leftover.<p>I found the seat belt latches I needed in a salvage yard along with a handful of other little interior parts and fasteners. It was a blazing hot day, and I took them all off myself, so the guy only charged me $10 total. <p>Thanks to Ogre's suggestion, I took the door panel off and put big oversized washers behind the remote mirror adjuster to keep it from popping up and warping the top of the panel. It look like it's supposed to again. I'm taking LowCG's advice and replacing the front lower ball joints with Cadillac units. This combined with slightly smaller tires will lower the front nose over an inch.<p>Replacing all the worn out rubber suspension bushings with polyurethane will be done in the near future. Relocating the battery from the back to the front is a top priority. I'm going to add a front air dam and rear sway bar too.<p>I'm in the middle of installing one of those special Pioneer CD player that fits into the factory dash location and putting in some new speakers. And just for kicks, I'm mounting a small Rockford-Fosgate Punch series amp and subwoofer behind the seats.<p>I also have the dash torn completely apart, replacing the gauges with Auto Meter Sport-Comp electric gauges (in dash 120 mph speedo and 8,000 rpm tach). I'm not sure where to put the volt/water/oil gauges yet, maybe in gauge cups on top of the center console (above the temp control).<p>With lots of help and ideas from the Fiero Forum, hard earned cash, hard work, and a few skinned knuckles, My batmobile is going to be one awesome ride.
Z||000001||theogre||07-11-99||07:11 PM||||you been one busy batty, thats for sure. Glad the mirror tip worked.... I figured it was the problem, but I ain't had time to test it..... mine has the same problem.... looks like shit. But I also have a mirror snagged door to fix.... I think I'm just going to get another door frame. It only takes a little longer than trying to fix the top beam in the end. (Mirror snagged from behind... it bent the beam. yuck.)
Z||000002||batboy||07-17-99||10:13 PM||||I just finished the Grand Am brake swap on the rear of my batmobile. This completes the brake conversion, because I had already done the front (see beginning of thread). One of the original rear caliper's mounting bolt was completely loose and I found a couple other things needing repaired while I had it torn down and up on jackstands.<p>The brakes are great and work very nice, much better than previously. This is the way Fiero brakes should be like from the factory. For the time being, I'm leaving the original master cylinder on. There's nothing wrong with it and it seems to work very good with the 1988 Grand Am calipers and rotors.<p>As mentioned earlier, I spent a little extra to buy new U.S.A. made rotors, OEM remanufactured calipers, flexible hoses, Raybestos semi-metallic pads, and new wheel bearings. Yet, the entire swap was about the same price as just a set of cross-drilled rotors from the Fiero Store. I'm very happy.<p>I installed my new Pioneer CD player and speakers, it sounds great. I don't have the amp or sub yet, but I've got an eight inch subwoofer box and all the wires run (except the amp power cable).<p>I got a side air scoop mailed to me from Bruce (a Forum regular). I had to do some modification to the scoop in order for it to fit right, but it sure looks good on the car now. Thanks Bruce.<p>The next step is new gauges. I should be getting my Auto Meter Sport-Comp instruments from Jegs any day now. I'm going to attempt to mount the aftermarket speedo and tach into the factory gauge cluster. I'll keep y'all posted on how this next project turns out.
Z||000003||batboy||07-25-99||07:28 PM||||Well...I got my Auto Meter speedo and tach, but it appears to be nearly impossible to mount them into the OEM instrument cluster. The main problem is that they are too deep. I'm back to square one there.<p>I took the batmobile on a road trip into town to run some errands. Got back into it and the car wouldn't start, finally figured out that the positive battery cable was bad. Walked to a parts store and got a new cable. I put the new cable on in upper 90 degree temps and high humidity with the sun frying my brain. Then, on the way home a radiator hose blew out. I guess this is what's called, "working the bugs out."<p>I did finally get my water temp gauge working again though, it was a bad sending unit. I also got the amp and sub mounted in. At least now when I break down, I can listen to some good tunes.
Z||000004||DJRice||07-26-99||06:57 AM||||Sounds like we should call you BatMechanic..... I got a new set of tires about 3 weeks ago, and since Sears gives free brake inspections I decided to take them up on it while it was on the rack.  Nautrally, they wanted to sell me $600 of a brake job (yeah, right!) One of the suggestions they made is replacement of the flex hoses and I was wondering, how much did you drop on the hoses?  This brake upgrade thing is very tempting.
Z||000005||batboy||07-26-99||10:32 AM||||Without going to the trouble of finding all my receipts, I believe the flex brake hoses were about $15 to $20 a piece. It seems like the rear ones cost a bit more than the front ones. I also had to order the rear ones since nobody seems to carry them in stock.<p>I do my best to work on my own cars and street rods, and have been doing so since the early 70's, but I still don't consider myself a real mechanic. I have an old high school buddy that runs his own shop, now that guy is a real whiz. I guess after 20 years of wrenching 10 hours a day 6 days a week, anybody would get good like him too.
Z||000006||batboy||08-01-99||09:02 PM||||Time for another batmobile update. I'm not sure anybody really cares, but I guess I'll continue until somebody tells me to quit boring them. I reckon you don't have to read this if you don't want to.<p>A couple posts ago, I mentioned that I got the Auto Meter Sport-Comp speedo and tach, but it wouldn't fit into the Fiero factory instrument cluster. Well...after spending a couple hours measuring and thinking, I decided to give it another try. I figured heck, I've modified and engineered harder things then this in a couple of my streetrods in the past.<p>After spending the better part of this weekend drilling, cutting, grinding, sweating, tweaking, cussing, and modifying, but I got the job done. I worked slow and trial fitted everything together about a hundred times as I progressed. If I would of ruined anything, I would have been in deep guano. It wasn't easy, but it sure looks nice with those gauges sitting in the dash now.<p>My neighbor stopped over as I was admiring my handiwork and said, "Wow, looks good! You were lucky to find those gauges that fit right in!" I just laughed and explained that they really didn't just fit right in, even though it may look like they do now.<p>I did not get the wiring done yet. The instructions that came with the speedo were way too vague about which colored wires went where. I have to call the tech line tomorrow. You would think they could provide a clear wiring diagram, sheesh!<p>Greendano sold me an auxilary gauge pod that sits on top of the center temp/radio console. I have that about half mounted on, but still have to figure out the wiring for that too. Hopefully by next week's batmobile update, I'll have a complete set of functioning gauges and instruments. See you next bat time on this same bat thread.
Z||000007||Patrick||08-01-99||10:25 PM||||<br>Hey batboy, continue to keep us posted. I enjoy reading your exploits and I'm sure others do too.<p>Funny, with the tag you've given yourself, I always thought you were some kid!<p><p>
Z||000008||batboy||08-01-99||11:58 PM||||Aren't we all kids at heart? Even us 42 year old boys with graying hair. :)
Z||000009||Racer 1||08-02-99||12:36 AM||||Hey batboy your a cool guy.Hey how much was the grand-am brake conversion?And did you have to buy the calipers and rotors only or did you have to get the front hub carrier.And will the grand-am brakes fit inside 14 inch rims?E-Mail me a pic if you can at "fiero_rob@yahoo.com" of the brakes im intrested on doing this mod!Thanks
Z||000010||batboy||08-02-99||06:06 AM||||Racer 1, total cost for the whole 1988 Grand Am to 1985 Fiero brake swap, front and rear, is approximately $400 which includes American made rotors and OEM rebuilt calipers which cost more money. I compared the Chinese rotors with the American rotors and decided to spend the extra $10 more each, there was a visible difference in quality. I also replaced all the wheel bearings, flexible brake hoses, and bought premium semi-metallic brake pads. A guy could do this a lot cheaper if he really wanted to. Lastly, I'm keeping my old calipers as backups for my other Fiero, so I didn't have any core returns ($10 per caliper). If I would have bought all of the same Fiero brake parts and rebuilt it all back to original, the cost probably would of been over $500.<p>You have to have the original front rotor machined down into a hub to allow the Grand Am/Baretta rotor to bolt up to it. The rear brakes were a bolt on, but you lose the parking brake (check local laws). I'm using 14 inch wheels, so yes they will fit. The Grand Am rotor is the same diameter (9.75 inches) as the old Fiero ones, but they are thicker and are vented. I took a couple pictures of the swap, but the film is still sitting in my camera undeveloped. Check out the below listed URL for pictures and a detail explanation of the swap.<p>http://members.tripod.com/Randy_Agee/brakes2.html<br>
Z||000011||Racer 1||08-02-99||11:56 AM||||Thanks Batboy I read the article Randy agee.Im gonna stay stock for now.Also would the grand-am brake rotor fit right on a 88 gt's front?pisa corp makes a front conversion kit that take a 84 - 87 fiero up to a 88's brakes.I might be able just to buy the front hubs of pisa and skip the machining part.
Z||000012||batboy||08-02-99||07:03 PM||||Racer 1, I saw those hubs at PISA after I was done with my brake swap and wondered the same thing. If you are planning on racing this Fiero, you may want to consider some of the more expensive high performance brake kits. I talked to Chris West from West Coast Fiero and he seemed to think the Grand Am brake swap was a waste of time for racing. However, I think my brake swap works great on the street. A major improvement over the factory equipment. If you don't want to lose your parking brake, you could do a swap on the front only and then run a adjustable proportioning valve. Chris West claims you can remove the OEM valve entirely if you upgrade the front brakes. I don't know about that. Check out this URL for brake upgrades (but I don't think you can use 14 inch wheels with those bigger rotors):<p>http://www.ryanemotorsports.com/brakes.htm
Z||000013||batboy||08-02-99||07:07 PM||||Oh yeah, about the 1988 brakes, I don't know if they will fit or not. I'm guessing probably not. I think that this Grand Am swap basically improves the 84-87 brakes up to the 88 level. I've never actually got to examine the 88 brakes, but I know that they are different.
Z||000014||lowCG||08-03-99||01:21 AM||||Hey Batboy,did you try any other rotors from other vehicles using the Fiero bolt pattern?<br>I found that Chrysler fwd.,Toyota Camry,Celica,VW Golf III all use the same pattern.<br>Also,Iroc Camaro rear calipers have E-brake stuff,and use vented rotors(like Grand Am)if you ever need an E-brake.<br>Thanks for the tip on the racers recomendations,still don't know what I'll be doing in this department,but my new(VW)springs,balljoints(Caddy),and Bilsteins just came and that'll keep me busy/broke for awhile.
Z||000015||batboy||08-03-99||06:02 AM||||LowCG, no I didn't try any other brakes, I have heard that there is a way to adapt and use S-10 brakes on the front too. Some of the canyon racers on the west coast are doing that. The rotors and calipers are larger than the Grand Am brakes. The IROC rear brakes sound like they would be worth looking into also. Maybe next time I go to the boneyard, I'll look around more carefully at some of the other GM cars brake setups.
Z||000016||lowCG||08-03-99||06:33 PM||||Well I'll have to look at some S-10 stuff,makes sense,wonder how much larger they are than the Grand Am setup,both vehicles came with 14" wheels.Always have to be researching the next project while I stumble through the current projects.
Z||000017||batboy||08-03-99||07:22 PM||||LowCG, it's the S-10 4X4 brakes they are using. Those trucks had 15 inch wheels as standard equipment compared to the 2 wheel drive models. I need to double check this as I'm not 100% sure, but I think those S-10 4X4 rotors are 10.25 inch diameter compared to the Grand Am and Fiero's 9.75 inch diameter. I'm pretty sure the caliper pistons are bigger too. I believe you have to engineer a caliper bracket and lose the dust shield in order for it to work. I wish I had better instructions than that, but I don't.
Z||000018||RICK||08-05-99||07:45 PM||||Bat..Please tell me your taking pictures of this as you go.  You know I'm waiting to see how your's turn out before I start tearing into mine.  <br>Do you live around Kansas City?
Z||000019||lowCG||08-05-99||07:53 PM||||Well that pretty much tells me everything,since fabrication is required anyways.Thanks,I will take a look at that stuff.
Z||000020||batboy||08-05-99||08:40 PM||||Rick, I have been taking a few pics with my trusty old fashioned Nikon, but I haven't got the film developed yet. I'm going to start borrowing a Sony digital camera, probably starting this weekend, and take a bunch of photos. Just last night I got my first home page web site set up. All that's on it right now is a message saying that it's under construction, but I'm planning on have a section devoted to my batmobile eventually. When I get something Fiero related onto my web page, I'll post the URL. By the way, I work out of Topeka, but I live a fair distance SW of there (a couple hours drive from KC). Email me, if you have any instructor openings at your school for geology or environmental science (I have a M.S. degree). Thank you for your support.
